<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Levant</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Levantine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Levant is the region along the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, roughly corresponding to present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, Israel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and Jordan. Its position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> made it a crossroads of trade and a frequent battleground. Empires including the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> competed to control it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/levant.jpg" alt="Levant" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Winkpolve · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Levant_(orthographic_projection).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
